CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 3 --por --signed-overflow-check
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
Two threads increment `x` under a mutex; after both joins it is 2.

With --signed-overflow-check, `count + 1` in the pthread_mutex_lock model
gets an overflow ASSERT inside the lock's atomic section. ASSERT and ASSUME
steps carry no atomic_section_id, so lazy_po took this one for a check
outside any atomic section and gave it a block of its own, which split the
section: another thread could run between the lock's write of its owner and
of its count, and both held the mutex at once. Without the option the same
program is SUCCESSFUL before and after.

Reported FAILED before the fix, SUCCESSFUL after. Reduced from
pthread-complex/workstealqueue_mutex-2 (no-overflow), which the fix also
corrects.

See `atomic_section_check_inside_real`: one thread increments without the
mutex, and that must stay FAILED.
